.RI ( thick
passed to
.BR G -style
lines).  Tessellated on the Metal path.
.PP
The Inferno Cocoa emu presents the softscreen with Metal.  Letters
.B g
/
.B k
(fillpoly3),
.B G
(line3),
.B h
(plot3),
.B j
(sprites), and
.B q
(circles) are queued as GPU primitives with per-vertex depth.
When
.B u
enables the z-buffer bit, a GPU depth test
.I sorts
fills, lines, sprites, and circles relative to each other;
.B z
clears that depth attachment.  GPU depth values are a monotonic map of
eye
.B z
chosen as an order-preserving mapping of the software plane depth.  They are
.I not
bit-identical to the software
.IR polyfill (2)
fixed-point
.B ZSCALE
plane buffer, but preserve its ordering across the full depth range.  Thick
.B G
lines
.RI ( thick >0)
are drawn as screen-space quads (Plan 9 width
.IR 1+2×thick ).
Filled polygons are fan-triangulated from vertex 0 (correct for the
convex faces used by Temple/Castle ports).  Concave ear-clip is
intentionally omitted; callers with concave polygons must tessellate
themselves or stay on the soft path.
.PP
Compositing: if ordinary 2D drawing follows queued 3D in the same frame,
the emu snapshots the softscreen at that transition, draws the snapshot,
then Metal 3D (including
.B j
sprites and
.B q
circles/ellipses), then overlays softscreen pixels that changed after the
snapshot (wm chrome, minimap, text HUD, and any soft fallback draws).
GPU drawing remains presentation-only while queued, but an
.B r
image-read request first renders pending geometry into an off-screen Metal
target and copies the result back to the softscreen.  Consequently
.IR readpixels (2)
observes all preceding draw3d operations in protocol order.  Obscured
layers and non-solid ops fall back to software
.B memfillpoly
/
.B memline
/
.B d3fillpolyz
(with the same depth ordering when z is enabled).  Software sprite fallback
also applies billboard scaling and Z rotation before mask-aware per-pixel depth
testing.
Other platforms keep software rasterisation for both.
.HP
.B y
.IR id [4]
.IR r [4*4]
.IR buf [x*1]
.br
.ti -0.5i
.B Y
.IR id [4]
.IR r [4*4]
.IR buf [x*1]
.br
Replace the rectangle 
.I r
of pixels in image
.I id
with the pixel data in 
.IR buf .
The pixel data must be in the format dictated by
.IR id 's
image channel descriptor (see
.IR image (6)).
The 
.B y
message uses uncompressed data,
while the
.B Y
message uses compressed data. 
In either case, 
it is an error to include more data than necessary.
.ig
.PP
Reading the
.B colormap
returns the system color map used on 8-bit displays.
Each color map entry consists of a single line containing
four space-separated decimal strings.
The first is an index into the map, and the remaining three are
the red, green, and blue values associated with that index.
The color map can be changed by writing entries in the
above format to
the 
.B colormap
file.
Note that changing the system color map 
does not change the color map used for
calculations involving
.B m8
images, which is immutable.
..
.PP
The
.B refresh
file is read-only.
As windows owned by the client are uncovered,
if they cannot be refreshed by the server (such as when they have
refresh functions associated with them), a message is made available
on the
.B refresh
file reporting what needs to be repainted by the client.
The message has five decimal integers formatted as in the
.B ctl
message: the image id of the window and the coordinates of the rectangle
that should be refreshed.
.SS "Hardware image decode"
Writing
.EX
	decode \fIid\fP \fIpath\fP
.EE
to
.B video
decodes the named file into the pixels of image
.I id
using the platform's hardware decoder.
The file is read through the namespace, not opened by the host, so
anything this process can see can be decoded \(em including a file on a
mount from another machine \(em and the decoder is handed the encoded
bytes rather than a path.
The image is an ordinary draw image, so everything that can be done
with one works on the result; where it is texture-backed the decoded
pixels land in GPU-visible memory without passing through the Limbo
heap.
The destination must be 32-bit, and the image is scaled to fit it, so a
caller need not know the file's dimensions in advance.
.PP
It is a file of its own rather than another verb on
.BR ctl ,
whose write is already a four-byte image id, and rather than a new
letter in the draw message protocol on
.BR data :
this is textual control, and it belongs beside the data the way
.IR audio (3)
separates
.B audioctl
from
.BR audio .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B INFERNO_TEXIMAGE
Allocate an image's pixels from GPU-visible memory, so that the
.B Memimage
and a texture are the same bytes rather than one being a copy of the
other.
On unified memory that removes the upload needed to composite an image,
and lets a hardware decoder write frames directly into an ordinary draw
image.
Only 32-bit images above a minimum size qualify \(em a mask or an 8-bit
